{
 "package": "./tools/review/sandbox/",
 "note": "The subject is tools/review/sandbox.sh (#988, item 4): the command that runs the tests of a pull request from outside the project in a throwaway Incus container instead of on the station. Exit 0: tests green; 2: tests red; 1: cannot judge.\n\nEach mutation removes one refusal, one limit of the box, one step of the order (network cut before the code enters), the trap that stops the box, or one class of control character the output escaper writes out. The tests run the real script against a real throwaway git repository and a stand-in `incus` (tools/review/sandbox/testdata/incus) that records every call and keeps just enough state to answer.\n\nWhat this does not falsify: that a real Incus behaves as the stand-in says (measured by hand on 2026-11-16 on Incus 7.6, see the pull request), that a pull request cannot escape the container through the kernel it shares with the station, that the order of the stages holds if a stage is moved (the tests prove the order on the calls that were made, not every reordering), and a pool that DOES apply a size: the stand-in answers what the box sees, only the dir pool of this station was measured (Incus 7.6 accepts size: on it and applies nothing, so the script refuses to run there).",
